Abstract = {`Welfare chauvinism' (or `welfare ethnocentrism,' when directed against
   native-born ethnic minorities) is a declination of nativism within the
   social policy domain and a common element of populist radical right
   discourse. Previous studies have shown that this rhetoric can influence
   how people perceive the deservingness and entitlement of certain groups
   to welfare rights. In this study, we propose it has additional effects
   by evoking a purported lack of reciprocity in what concerns benefits
   from, and contributions to, the welfare system, such rhetoric can also
   justify and legitimize discrimination against out-groups in other
   domains that are unrelated to welfare. We use a pre-registered
   experiment embedded in a survey of a nationally representative sample of
   the Portuguese population to examine whether individuals who are exposed
   to the issue of illegitimate `takers' of the welfare system become more
   likely to express discriminatory intentions regarding an out-group's
   freedom of movement and establishment. We find that in the Portuguese
   context, where the populist radical right frequently portrays the Roma
   minority as welfare abusers, highlighting the issue of reciprocity can
   trigger a sizeable increase in discriminatory intentions against the
   Romani even in domains unrelated to welfare rights.},
